# Mutation cases for "a lane slower than the push gap is not superseded".
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/a_lane_that_cannot_finish_is_still_superseded.cases`.
#
# `ci_supersession_policy` decides one literal per workflow — whether a new
# run may cancel the one already in flight — by DERIVING it from a measured
# median in `LANES`. Nothing watched that derivation, and on 2026-09-02 the
# cost came due: `tree-hygiene.yml` had grown from 9.9 to 20.1 minutes against
# the 18.9 minute push gap the table then carried, its row still read 9.9, and
# so the rule went on approving `cancel-in-progress: true`. Both of those
# figures were themselves re-taken hours later, at 20.5 and 17.6, and the
# conclusion did not move -- they are recorded here as the readings of that
# day, not as the ones in force.
#
# Twelve of its last twenty-five runs
# were cancelled and every one of them killed a job that had ALREADY STARTED,
# 68s to 1264s in. Ten were consecutive, and among the ten was the commit
# repairing a red this same lane had raised.
#
# What made it last four days is a circle: `ci_supersession_policy` RUNS IN
# `tree-hygiene.yml`. While the lane is being superseded, the check that would
# say "move this row" is the thing being superseded. A guard a defect can
# silence needs a reader outside that loop, which is what these cases are.
#
# The second case is the one that keeps the first honest. The first proves the
# rule fires when a long lane declares `true`; it proves nothing if the reader
# cannot tell `true` from `false`, because then every lane reads alike and the
# assertion is about a constant rather than about the file. Case two removes
# the distinction and requires the suite to notice.
#
# ⚠ Case one anchors on a live literal, which the paced-cost casefile next
# door was bitten by. The difference is the failure mode, not the shape: that
# map is DESIGNED to empty, so its anchor's death was routine, while this
# literal only moves if somebody re-measures the lane back under the gap — a
# deliberate act that must edit `LANES` in the same commit. And a dead anchor
# here is loud, not silent: `scripts/mutate` calls it INCONCLUSIVE and
# `scripts/gate mutation-cases` turns that into a red.

mutation_tests -p sce-build --test ci_supersession_policy
mutation_targets sce-build/tests/ci_supersession_policy.rs
# Read at run time rather than compiled in: the literal under judgement lives
# in the workflow file, which the test opens by path.
mutation_runtime_targets .github/workflows/tree-hygiene.yml

mutation_case "the lane that outgrew the push gap may cancel its own run again" <<'PY'
edit(".github/workflows/tree-hygiene.yml",
     "  cancel-in-progress: false",
     "  cancel-in-progress: true")
PY

mutation_case "the reader cannot tell a declared true from a declared false" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/ci_supersession_policy.rs",
     '"true" => Some(true),',
     '"true" => Some(false),')
PY

# The next two come in a pair on purpose. A long lane passes the rule on
# `cancel-in-progress: false` OR on a per-commit concurrency group, and a
# disjunction has two ways to rot: the second arm can stop recognising the
# mechanism it is for, or it can start recognising it everywhere and swallow
# the first arm whole. One case each, from the two sides.

mutation_case "a per-commit group is read as an ordinary shared one" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/ci_supersession_policy.rs",
     'g.contains("github.sha")',
     'g.contains("github.no-such-context")')
PY

mutation_case "every group is read as per-commit, so the flag stops mattering" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/ci_supersession_policy.rs",
     'g.contains("github.sha")',
     'g.contains("github.")')
PY

# The window is 25 runs, so the three outcome columns cannot sum past it. This
# is the shape a half-updated row takes: one column re-taken, the rest left.
mutation_case "a row's columns are taken from different readings" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/ci_supersession_policy.rs",
     '("cpp-suite.yml", 79.1, 12, 11, 2),',
     '("cpp-suite.yml", 79.1, 12, 20, 2),')
PY

# ...and the same check must actually SUM the fifth column. While the row was
# two columns wide this passed on `mutation-rounds.yml` reporting 11 outcomes
# out of 25, because the fourteen unstarted runs were in no column at all.
# `clang-format-check.yml` is chosen so the raised count stays BELOW its
# successes: that keeps the backlog invariant below out of it, so the red can
# only come from the window arithmetic.
mutation_case "the unfinished column is left out of the window sum" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/ci_supersession_policy.rs",
     '("clang-format-check.yml", 0.8, 0, 25, 0),',
     '("clang-format-check.yml", 0.8, 0, 25, 5),')
PY

# A backlog larger than the lane's successes is reachable only under a
# per-commit group -- a shared group has the next push clear whatever is still
# pending. So a row claiming one on a shared group contradicts its own
# workflow. There is no violating lane today, which is exactly why the case
# has to construct one; `ecma262-lowered-kotlin.yml` has six successes, so
# seven unfinished trips the invariant while the window sum stays at 13.
#
# ⚠ No companion case blinds the reader from the other side, and the reason is
# stated rather than hidden: with no lane violating the invariant, widening or
# disabling the comparison changes no verdict and produces no red. A case that
# cannot fail is not evidence, so none is written.
mutation_case "a shared-group lane claims a backlog only a sha key can make" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/ci_supersession_policy.rs",
     '("ecma262-lowered-kotlin.yml", 6.3, 0, 6, 0),',
     '("ecma262-lowered-kotlin.yml", 6.3, 0, 6, 7),')
PY

# The last three are about the ENGLISH copies of the same two numbers. Both
# figures are also written out in the workflow each row is about, and moving
# the constant from 18.9 to 17.6 left seven of those copies behind -- a
# rationale still claiming a derivation it no longer had. These ask whether
# the copies now have an owner.
#
# ⚠ Neither of the first two may change a classification, or the red would
# come from the older assertion and prove nothing about the new ones. 17.7
# still sits below `deploy-visualizer` at 17.9 and above `embed-vendor-smoke`
# at 15.2, and 70.1 leaves `w3c-tests` long exactly as 70.0 does.

mutation_case "the constant moves and the prose copies keep the old number" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/ci_supersession_policy.rs",
     "const MEDIAN_PUSH_GAP_MINUTES: f64 = 17.6;",
     "const MEDIAN_PUSH_GAP_MINUTES: f64 = 17.7;")
PY

mutation_case "one row moves and its own workflow goes on stating the old one" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/ci_supersession_policy.rs",
     '("w3c-tests.yml", 70.0, 14, 9, 2),',
     '("w3c-tests.yml", 70.1, 14, 9, 2),')
PY

# A scanner that finds nothing agrees with everything, which is why both cases
# above would pass on a blinded reader. The floor is what refuses that, and
# this is the case that keeps the floor honest.
mutation_case "the reader stops recognising the phrase it owns" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/ci_supersession_policy.rs",
     'const CLAIM_PHRASE: &str = "min median";',
     'const CLAIM_PHRASE: &str = "min medians";')
PY
